illed is to ask the British  people to pay the price for the terrible  mistakes made from the mini budget just  just weeks ago so the ongoing cost of  chaos and instability in the Tory party  is still going to be paid by the public  no matter how many times they change the  faces at the top  but you you'll have seen this at the  figures the same as me the markets have  stabilized and he hasn't even become  Prime Minister yet I'm just asking why  Rishi Suna should not be given and some  form of grace period you're a few months  at least to see if you can actually deal  with the problems and if you're talking  about a general election it'll be  another three months or the best part of  another two months at the very least  before there would be a labor  Administration in place so why not just  give him the opportunity now to try and  sort out the country  because I think the the longer this goes  on this uh Merry-Go-Round of changing  the Prime Minister changing the  chancellor every month and all these  changes the further you get away from uh  the election result in 2019 what is the  policy Now is it going to be the policy  of 2019 is it going to be that remember  I do remember Gordon Brown in 2017.  yeah I do remember Gordon Brown back in  2007 resisting calls to calls for an  election and you know the financial  situation wasn't brilliant back then  either  yeah look it does happen that prime  minister will change during the course  of a parliament but this is the third  time in three months we've not had a  situation like that before nor have we  had a situation where we've had four  chancellors in four months uh nor have  we had a situation where with such a  self-inflicted wound as that many budget  a few weeks ago so I think there are  arguments but look in the end the  Constitutional position about this is  that with a big Tory majority the only  way there'll be an election is if the  new prime minister agrees to one so much  as I'd like to see one he probably won't  have one and I think that's been the  indication over the last 24 hours  do you know what Miss McFadden I think  you might have a point on that one um I  do want to just conclude though with the  way that this battle between the  government benches and the opposition  benches will be fought as we move  forward we are everyone assumes about to  enter you know austerity 2.0 in  political terms Rishi sunak will be the  face alongside his Transit of course but  he will be the face of some pretty  significant cuts that are going to be  made do you think that someone who is  worth hundreds of millions of pounds can  adopt that role  I don't think his personal wealth has  got anything to do with it I don't  really like that kind of uh Politics as  long as the policies are fair and to to  pick up a new phrase austerity 2.0 why  is that no one was talking about public  expenditure Cuts before the mini budget  uh of September the 23rd and what's  going to happen now is that the British  people are going to be asked to pay the  price for the Tories political and  economic mistakes that's actually why we  should have an election that's why we  should have a change of government  because this party after 12 years in  power is now tired it's clapped out it's  divided it doesn't have a plan for the  future so we can have a new prime  minister today and a new cabinet today  but they do not have any new ideas as to  how to take the country forward  apparently found in great to have you on  the program this morning thanks for  being with us
 

